{"id":"o-haas-lilienthalhouse-org","claimKey":"dd7339b30dbfdf98ef719fe5ceb5c21c2c27d424280ed84d4e47c90dfd56425f","title":"Haas-Lilienthal House","cat":"play","art":"museum","area":"San Francisco, CA","metroId":"san-francisco","src":"haas-lilienthalhouse.org","specs":[],"options":[{"name":"Tickets","detail":"Standard","price":150},{"name":"Rentals","detail":"Same-Day Early Access","price":200},{"name":"Rentals","detail":"Day-Before Access","price":350}],"services":[{"name":"Tickets","desc":null,"variants":[{"label":"Standard","price":150,"optionIdx":0}]},{"name":"Rentals","desc":"In the 19th and 20th centuries, it was common for a prosperous middle class family to have a number of paid servants, or staff, most of whom both lived and worked in the home. The Haas and Lilienthal families had close to 6-7 people in their employment at any given time. Their staff was comprised of a chauffeur, an upstairs maid, a downstairs maid, a nursemaid (nanny), a laundryman, a cook and a waitress. Census records show that the household staff was comprised of immigrants from Nova Scotia, China, Japan, Ireland, Germany, and France. Tillie (Otilia) Richter (1893-1975) served as the cook at 2007 Franklin St. for 34 years from, 1938 to 1972. She immigrated to the United States from","variants":[{"label":"Same-Day Early Access","price":200,"optionIdx":1},{"label":"Day-Before Access","price":350,"optionIdx":2}]}],"includes":[],"addons":[],"gap":"Prices, hours and eligibility are not copied here yet. We will ask when you request.","cover":"https://static.wixstatic.com/media/5a7737_9f45637d44664470acc3b0c8c431fc2f%7Emv2.png/v1/fill/w_1600,h_1000,al_c,q_85/5a7737_9f45637d44664470acc3b0c8c431fc2f~mv2.png","photos":["https://static.wixstatic.com/media/5a7737_9f45637d44664470acc3b0c8c431fc2f%7Emv2.png/v1/fill/w_1600,h_1000,al_c,q_85/5a7737_9f45637d44664470acc3b0c8c431fc2f~mv2.png","https://static.wixstatic.com/media/7b7969_07a531b407be4cfb96e90e00b9531054~mv2.png/v1/fill/w_1600,h_1000,al_c,q_85/7